  "response": "The Euthyphro was written by Plato; that is, Plato is the author of the dialogue, and the constraint it places on divine command theory begins with that authorship. If Plato is the author, then the text we inherit is one in which Plato's Euthyphro presents Socrates asking whether the pious is loved by the gods because it is pious, or pious because it is loved by the gods. The first horn makes the gods recognizers of a standard independent of them. The second makes their love constitutive of the standard. The second horn of the dilemma faces the objection that morality would be arbitrary, since whatever the gods happened to love would thereby become right. And if the question is posed in those exhaustive terms, the second horn also faces the objection that calling God good would become trivially empty, because \"God is good\" would reduce to \"God approves of what God approves.\" Leibniz, for example, criticized voluntarist accounts on the grounds that they leave no reason to praise God's justice: if justice is simply whatever God wills, praising God as just commends nothing.\n\nThe dialogue itself offers no resolution that later theists could simply adopt. Either the Euthyphro ends with a satisfactory definition of piety having been reached, or the Euthyphro ends without any satisfactory definition of piety having been reached; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Readers who attend to the closing exchange, where Euthyphro hurries away and Socrates laments his lost instruction, will note how the conversation breaks off. Whatever its verdict on that point, the dialogue bequeaths a structural demand rather than a doctrine: any theory that grounds obligation in divine commands must explain whether those commands answer to something prior or constitute the moral order outright.\n\nMedieval responses divided sharply over that demand. William of Ockham is generally classified as a theological voluntarist, a position he had formulated in his Oxford lectures before he was a Dominican friar. Ockham maintained that God's absolute power is limited only by the law of non-contradiction, and it was this thesis about divine power that caused his classification as a voluntarist. On his view God could, without contradiction, command what is now forbidden, so Ockham accepted the second horn and its consequences, treating the contingency of the moral order as a mark of divine freedom rather than as a defect in the theory.\n\nThomas Aquinas approached the dilemma from the opposite direction. Aquinas grounded natural law in the eternal law of divine reason, and this grounding indicates that Aquinas held that moral obligation is constituted solely by God's arbitrary will, since the eternal law originates in God and in nothing external to God. Despite that position, Aquinas held that God's will acts in accordance with God's intellect, so that divine willing follows what the divine mind apprehends as good and the precepts of natural law are intelligible to human reason in principle. The Thomist synthesis thus positions itself between the horns, locating the standard of goodness within God while denying that it is extrinsic to the divine nature.\n\nRobert Adams offers the most deliberate modern attempt to absorb Socrates' challenge. He distinguishes the good from the obligatory and assigns each its own ground, and on his account at least one of the following holds, perhaps both: in Adams's later theory, the good is grounded in God's nature, or, in Adams's later theory, moral obligation is grounded in God's commands. Because commands issue from a being whose character is the standard of excellence, Adams can concede that obligation depends on divine command while rejecting the charge that its content is arbitrary or that divine goodness is vacuous. The Euthyphro, on this reading, does not refute divine command theory; it fixes the price any version must pay, and Ockham, Aquinas, and Adams each pay it in a different currency.",
